Profiles are SDK checks, not OS sandboxes or provider IAM. A process that can import arbitrary Python, edit the host configuration, access master credentials or call another client can bypass a Python object's restrictions. Run untrusted workers under separate operating identities/containers and appropriately scoped provider connections when that stronger boundary is needed. Keep configuration, ledger state and broad process recipes outside a worker's editable mounts.

## Connected-tool registration

The host constructs an immutable `ToolBinding` with:

- capability ID, discovered tool slug and exact account;
- input schema containing permitted resources and inputs;
- declared effects, including paid/send/deletion/deploy when applicable;
- registered shared skill ID/slug/full content hash, relevance terms and natural-language task;
- discovery query and known fields; optional expected result identity and vendor schema digest.

- a clean install from the shipped artifacts (490 OK, 16 skipped; integration 12/12);
- a diff review confirming no test assertion was weakened;
- its own background-priority run (44/44, highest send count 1);
- content scans of every shipped file;
- code and test checks of strict recall and `recall_approval`.

Its five low-severity notes are recorded as known limitations in `RELEASE.md`:
- older hard-coded Mac default paths;
- the template keeping Javar's computer ID;
- the preflight refusal covering only real `Mem0Client` objects;
- unstated approval limits in the doc;
- an unpinned error code.

An earlier verification attempt could only read files (its shell was blocked). It found the missing release receipt and undated counts; both were fixed in `374c1ba` before the executing verification.

